        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "c798d198-80da-5eb8-8074-45f07fd11b45",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Organizations: Collective for Living Cinema\n- People: Dziga Vertov, Jonathan Demme, Lev Kuleshov, Sergei Eisenstein, Vsevolod Pudovkin, Aleksandr Dovzhenko\n- Places: White Street, Lower Manhattan, New York City, United States of America\n- Events: Made in Texas - New Films From Austin series\n- Concepts: avant-garde cinema, Soviet documentary film, newsreels, cinema theory, Italian futurism in cinema, Russian Futurist cinema, German Expressionism\n\nFacts:\n- Dziga Vertov, born David Abelevich Kaufman and also known as Denis Kaufman, was born on 2 January 1896 and became a Soviet pioneer documentary film and newsreel director as well as a cinema theorist.\n- Italian futurism in cinema influenced Russian Futurist cinema, including Lev Kuleshov, Dziga Vertov, Sergei Eisenstein, Vsevolod Pudovkin, and Aleksandr Dovzhenko, and it also influenced German Expressionism.\n- Dziga Vertov died on 12 February 1954.\n- In October 1981, director Jonathan Demme presented the Made in Texas - New Films From Austin series at the Collective for Living Cinema in New York City.\n- The Collective for Living Cinema was an outpost of avant-garde cinema located on White Street in Lower Manhattan in the United States of America.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
